✅ 31. Print ABC Sequentially Using 3 Threads (A → B → C)

🎗️ 31.1 Definition
The ABC Cyclic Pattern is a multithreading synchronization problem where three threads must print A, B, and C in strict cyclic order.

The required sequence is:
A → B → C → A → B → C → A → B → C ...

For exactly N repetitions.
Example

If: N = 3
Expected output: ABCABCABC

❌ Invalid Outputs

🔴 AABBCC
🔴 AAABBBCCC
🔴 ABCCBA
🔴 ACBACB

✔ Only valid sequence
🔴 ABCABCABC

The important point is that three independent threads must coordinate with each other to maintain one strict sequence.

🎗️ 31.2 Problem Statement

We have three threads:

🔴 Thread-A
🔴 Thread-B
🔴 Thread-C

Each thread has only one responsibility.

Thread-A
   ↓
Print A

Thread-B
   ↓
Print B

Thread-C
   ↓
Print C

🔴 The threads must cooperate to produce: ABCABCABC... for exactly N repetitions.

🎗️ 31.3 Threads

There are exactly three threads.

Thread	Responsibility
Thread-A	Print A
Thread-B	Print B
Thread-C	Print C

The threads don't decide randomly when to print.
Instead, they follow a shared state.

Thread-A
    ↓
    A
    ↓
Thread-B
    ↓
    B
    ↓
Thread-C
    ↓
    C
    ↓
Thread-A

Therefore: A → B → C → A → B → C


🎗️ 31.4 Why Is This Asked?

This is a very common thread synchronization interview problem.
The interviewer wants to check whether you understand how multiple threads can coordinate when there is no numerical condition such as:

number % 2
or:
number % 3
or:
number % threadCount

Compare

Odd-Even: number % 2
Remainder Problem: number % 3
ABC Pattern: No number condition

Instead, we have: Current Turn

👉 The question becomes:
Which thread is allowed to execute now?
The answer is determined by the shared turn state.

