🎗️ 31.19 Why wait()?

Suppose: turn = B and Thread-A gets the monitor.
Thread-A checks: turn == A

Result: false

Thread-A cannot print.

It should not continuously check:

while(turn != A) {
    // keep checking
}

That would be busy waiting.

Instead:wait() is used.
The thread enters the waiting state.

🎗️ 31.20 What Happens When wait() Is Called?

When a synchronized thread calls wait():

Thread
   ↓
wait()
   ↓
WAITING
   ↓
Releases monitor
   ↓
Another thread can acquire monitor

This is important because otherwise the waiting thread could hold the monitor forever.

Example:

Thread-A
   ↓
turn != A
   ↓
wait()
   ↓
Monitor released
   ↓
Thread-B can execute

🎗️ 31.21 Why While Instead of If?

This is a very important interview question.

❌ Wrong approach
if(turn != A) {
    wait();
}
✔ Correct approach
while(turn != A) {
    wait();
}

Why?
Because after waking up, the thread must re-check the condition.
A thread waking up does not automatically mean: I am allowed to print.
It only means: I can compete for the monitor again.

Therefore:

wait()
   ↓
Wake Up
   ↓
Re-acquire monitor
   ↓
Check condition again
   ↓
Can I print?

If: Yes → Print

If: No → wait() again

🎗️ 31.22 Spurious Wakeups

A thread waiting with wait() can wake up without the desired state transition.
This is called a: Spurious Wakeup

Therefore, this pattern is required:

while(condition) {
    wait();
}

not:

if(condition) {
    wait();
}

The while loop guarantees that the state is validated again before the thread performs its action.

🎗️ 31.23 Why notifyAll()?

After a thread changes the shared state, waiting threads need to be informed.

Example:

Thread-A
   ↓
Print A
   ↓
turn = B
   ↓
notifyAll()

Now Thread-B can wake up and check: turn == B

Similarly:

Thread-B
   ↓
Print B
   ↓
turn = C
   ↓
notifyAll()

Then Thread-C can proceed.

Finally:

Thread-C
   ↓
Print C
   ↓
turn = A
   ↓
notifyAll()

Thread-A can proceed again.

🎗️ 31.24 Why notifyAll() After Every State Transition?

Because every print causes a state transition.
A → B requires notification.
B → C requires notification.
C → A requires notification.

Therefore:

Print A
   ↓
turn = B
   ↓
notifyAll()


Print B
   ↓
turn = C
   ↓
notifyAll()


Print C
   ↓
turn = A
   ↓
notifyAll()


🎗️ 31.25 Why notifyAll() Instead of notify()?

Suppose all three threads are involved.

At some point, multiple threads could be waiting.

Thread-A → WAITING
Thread-B → WAITING
Thread-C → RUNNING

If only:

notify()

is used, only one waiting thread is awakened.

That awakened thread might not be the thread whose turn it is.

With:

notifyAll()

all waiting threads are awakened.

Each thread then checks:

Is it my turn?

Therefore:

notifyAll()
      ↓
All waiting threads wake
      ↓
Each re-checks turn
      ↓
Correct thread proceeds

