🎗️ 31.49 Common Mistake — Calling run() Instead of start()

A multithreading problem requires actual thread execution.

Calling:

run()

does not create a new thread of execution.

Calling:

start()

starts the thread.

Conceptually:

start()
   ↓
New Thread
   ↓
run()

Therefore:

start()

should be used when launching Thread-A, Thread-B, and Thread-C.

🎗️ 31.50 Common Mistake — Printing Outside Turn Validation

Every thread must verify:

Is it my turn?

before printing.

For example, Thread-A must never print simply because it acquired the monitor.

It must verify:

turn == A

The monitor guarantees mutual exclusion.

The turn state guarantees correct ordering.

These are two different responsibilities.

🎗️ 31.51 synchronized vs turn

This distinction is extremely important in interviews.

synchronized

Answers:

