Answers:

Which thread is logically allowed to print now?

It provides ordering.

Therefore:

synchronized
      ↓
Mutual Exclusion


turn
      ↓
Execution Order

Both work together.

🎗️ 31.52 wait() vs notifyAll()

| Operation     | Purpose                               |
| ------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| `wait()`      | Put current thread into waiting state |
| `notifyAll()` | Wake waiting threads                  |
| `wait()`      | Releases monitor while waiting        |
| `notifyAll()` | Does not release monitor immediately  |
| `wait()`      | Used when thread cannot proceed       |
| `notifyAll()` | Used after state changes              |

Interview answer:

wait() is used when a thread cannot proceed and must release the monitor while waiting.
notifyAll() is used after changing the shared state to wake waiting threads so they can re-check their conditions.

🎗️ 31.53 wait() vs sleep()

| `wait()`                                | `sleep()`                          |
| --------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| Releases monitor                        | Does not release monitor           |
| Used for thread communication           | Used for delaying execution        |
| Must be called while owning the monitor | Does not require monitor ownership |
| Waits for notification/state change     | Waits for a specified duration     |
| Part of coordination                    | Part of timing/delay               |

Interview Answer

wait() releases the monitor while waiting, whereas sleep() does not release the monitor if the thread already owns it.

🎗️ 31.54 Busy Waiting vs wait()

| Busy Waiting                       | `wait()`                            |
| ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| Continuously checks condition      | Thread waits                        |
| Wastes CPU                         | More efficient                      |
| Keeps executing                    | Enters waiting state                |
| Poor synchronization approach      | Appropriate communication mechanism |
| No monitor release through waiting | Monitor is released by `wait()`     |

Conceptually:

Busy Waiting


