🎗️ 31.66 Interview Questions
Q1. How do you print ABC using three threads?

Answer:

Use a shared synchronization object with a turn variable representing A, B, or C. Each thread waits until its turn, prints its character, updates the turn to the next state, and notifies waiting threads.

Q2. Why do you need a turn variable?

Because the turn variable determines:

Which thread is allowed to print.

It enforces:

A → B → C → A
Q3. Why can't we simply start the three threads one after another?

Because:

start(A)
start(B)
start(C)

does not guarantee:

A → B → C

Thread scheduling is controlled by the JVM/OS.

Therefore explicit synchronization is required.

Q4. Why is count needed?

To determine how many complete:

ABC

cycles have been completed and when execution should terminate.

Q5. Why does C increment count?

Because:

ABC

is one complete cycle.

After A:

A

cycle incomplete.

After B:

AB

cycle incomplete.

After C:

ABC

cycle complete.

Therefore:

C → count++
Q6. Why use enum?

Because the state has exactly three possible values:

A
B
C

An enum represents these explicit states naturally.

Q7. Why not use Boolean?

Boolean represents only two states:

true
false

ABC requires three:

A
B
C

Therefore enum is more appropriate.

Q8. Why use while instead of if before wait()?

Because the condition must be checked again after waking.

Reasons include:

Spurious Wakeups
Multiple Waiting Threads
State Changes
Q9. Why use notifyAll()?

Because multiple threads may be waiting.

notifyAll() wakes all waiting threads, allowing each one to re-check whether it is now its turn.

Q10. What happens if we forget C → A?

The cyclic sequence breaks.

Correct transition:

C → A

is mandatory.

Q11. Can Semaphore solve ABC?

Yes.

Use separate semaphores to control:

A → B → C → A
Q12. Can ReentrantLock solve ABC?

Yes.

ReentrantLock with Condition objects can implement the same state machine.

Q13. Is this a race-condition problem?

It involves shared state and therefore requires synchronization to prevent unsafe concurrent access.

The synchronization also establishes the required ordering.

Q14. Does synchronized alone guarantee ABC order?

No.

synchronized provides mutual exclusion, but it does not by itself establish:

A → B → C

The turn state is responsible for ordering.

Q15. Does thread scheduling guarantee ABC?

No.

The JVM/OS scheduler decides when threads execute.

Explicit synchronization is required to guarantee the output sequence.

Q16. What happens if Thread-B executes before Thread-A?

Thread-B checks:

turn == B?

Initially:

turn = A

Therefore B cannot proceed and waits.

This preserves:

A → B
Q17. What happens if Thread-C executes first?

Initially:

turn = A

Thread-C checks:

turn == C

which is false.

Therefore C waits.

The same logic prevents C from printing before A and B.

Q18. What is the most important invariant?

Only the thread whose identity matches the current turn is allowed to print.

Q19. What represents one complete iteration?
ABC

not:

A

and not:

AB
Q20. What is the state transition?
A → B
B → C
C → A
